# Velmora Full Product Requirements

Velmora is a decentralized C and C++ package manager for sharing binary artifacts, resolving dependencies, and connecting package recipes to native build and development workflows.

### FP-01: Configuration and build profiles

- A caller can select an independent Velmora home, inspect its effective configuration, and reuse stored configuration and named profiles in subsequent invocations without affecting another home.

- A caller can install configuration from a local directory, an archive, a downloadable archive or a Git repository. Selected source and destination subdirectories and declared exclusions determine which supplied configuration files are installed; an unavailable source causes configuration installation to fail.

- A caller can list stored profiles, locate a selected profile, and inspect the composed settings, options, configuration and environment separately for the host and build contexts. A requested profile that does not exist is refused.

- Profiles can include other profiles and derive values from the caller-supplied environment. Including profiles override included profiles; later supplied profiles override earlier ones for the same setting or option, and explicit command-line assignments override profile assignments.

- The host profile describes the platform for the produced package and the build profile describes the platform executing build tools. Assignments with no explicit context apply to the host; callers can instead target the build context or both contexts.

- Package-pattern assignments apply only to matching packages. Matching package-specific settings override unscoped settings, and later matching assignments to the same setting override earlier ones.

- Settings are checked against the installed setting definitions, including caller-provided extensions. The supplied definitions accept Linux, Windows, Macos and FreeBSD operating-system selections; x86, x86_64 and armv8 architecture selections; and Debug, Release, RelWithDebInfo and MinSizeRel build configurations. Unknown settings and values outside a declared domain are refused. Missing settings required by a recipe make the package configuration invalid; graph inspection reports the invalid configuration and installation refuses it.

- Profile detection records the detected operating system, architecture and available compiler information as a starting profile. An existing profile is protected from replacement unless the caller explicitly requests replacement.

- When a configuration or recipe declares an accepted Velmora version range, an executing Velmora version outside that range is refused.

- When no profile is supplied for a context, its configured default profile is selected. A context-specific environment selection overrides the stored default selection. Without either customization, both contexts use the standard default profile; a missing default is refused rather than detected implicitly.

### FP-02: Recipe authoring and package declarations

- A caller can provide a Python recipe defining package behavior or a text consumer recipe declaring dependencies, build tools, options and requested build integration. A missing or unreadable recipe is refused.

- Recipe inspection exposes the declared package identity, descriptive metadata, settings, options and defaults without requiring a binary package. Recipe loading failures identify the affected recipe and the underlying error.

- A recipe can declare a generic library, static library, shared library, header library, application, build scripts, reusable Python recipe, configuration package or unknown package kind. For a generic library or an omitted kind, an enabled header-only choice selects a header library; otherwise a declared shared-library choice selects shared or static according to that choice. Without either choice, an omitted kind remains unknown and a generic library is refused as incomplete. An explicit concrete kind takes precedence over the header-only choice; an explicit static or shared kind contradicting the shared-library choice is refused as inconsistent.

- Recipe-declared option defaults apply when the caller supplies no overriding choice. Consumer choices override dependency defaults, and profile and command-line choices override consumer defaults. A choice outside the recipe-declared domain is refused as an invalid option. An assignment to an undeclared option is refused when directed to the consumer or an exact package reference; assignments using an all-package or variable-length wildcard selector ignore options absent from a matching package. An option left without a value makes the package configuration invalid unless its declared domain permits absence. Graph inspection reports this invalid configuration while installation refuses it.

- Recipes can declare package-wide and component-specific include locations, library locations, libraries, system libraries, compiler definitions, compile and link flags, frameworks and component requirements. Consumers receive the declarations for the selected package configuration. Package-relative locations are exposed to consumers relative to the selected package directory.

- Project creation produces a recipe and associated project material from caller-supplied template values. Built-in project families are a basic recipe, a header library, libraries and applications for CMake, Meson, MSBuild, Bazel, Autotools and Premake, a Qbs library, a local recipes index, and a workspace. A generated recipe of a library or application family resolves to a library or application package kind accordingly, and the header library family to a header library. In the workspace family, caller-supplied version values apply to the initial library member; other members retain the template’s supplied identities.

- Caller-provided templates can substitute values in both file names and text contents, while files explicitly excluded from substitution retain their contents. An unknown template or a required but undefined template value is refused; existing destination files are not overwritten without explicit replacement permission.

- A caller-selected local template takes precedence over an installed template of the same name, and an installed template takes precedence over a built-in template. Without a selected template, project creation supplies a basic recipe.

### FP-03: Recipe export and revision tracking

- Exporting a Python recipe with a package name and version makes that recipe available for later reference-based operations in local managed storage. Optional user and channel qualifiers distinguish otherwise identical recipe identities. Missing required identity information and malformed references are refused.

- Export includes the recipe and its declared exported files and exported source files, applying inclusion and exclusion patterns.

- With content-based recipe revisions, re-exporting unchanged exported content retains the recipe revision, while changing the exported recipe, exported files or exported source contents produces a distinct revision. Previously exported revisions remain independently selectable.

- A recipe can instead choose source-control-based revision identity, in which case its revision identifies the source-control commit used for the export. An unavailable source-control revision is reported as an export failure.

- An explicit recipe revision selects that revision. Without an update or lock constraint, an unpinned reference selects the latest locally registered revision; if absent locally, it selects the latest revision from the first eligible remote containing that recipe. An update of an unpinned recipe compares the latest revisions from the eligible remotes by their recorded timestamps and replaces a local revision only with a newer one.

### FP-04: Remote selection and authentication

- A caller can add, inspect, rename, update, reorder, enable, disable and remove named remotes. Changes persist across invocations. Adding an existing name without explicit replacement, renaming to an existing name, and selecting a nonexistent remote are refused.

- Repository operations use the selected enabled remotes in their configured order. Disabling a remote excludes it from ordinary repository selection without deleting its registration; local-only operation makes no remote repositories available for resolution.

- A remote can be restricted to recipes, in which case its recipes remain available but its binary packages are not candidates for installation. Package eligibility filters can contain inclusion and exclusion patterns. Any matching exclusion prevents eligibility, irrespective of pattern order; otherwise at least one inclusion must match when inclusions are present, and exclusion-only filters allow every reference not excluded.

- A caller can authenticate to individual remotes, inspect the associated user, and clear saved credentials. Saved credentials are shared by registrations of the same repository URL and are isolated between distinct repository URLs. Invalid credentials are reported as authentication failure; valid credentials lacking repository permission are reported as denied access.

- Non-interactive operations can obtain credentials from the supplied environment, with remote-specific credentials taking precedence over generic credentials. A required interactive response is refused when non-interactive operation is selected.

- Remote transport verifies server certificates by default. A caller can explicitly disable this verification for a selected remote; certificate verification failures with verification enabled are reported as transport failures.

### FP-05: Dependency resolution and recipe reuse

- Given a consumer recipe or direct requirements and host and build profiles, Velmora resolves direct and transitive dependencies and exposes their selected versions, recipe revisions, dependency relationships and contexts. Missing recipes are identified as unresolved requirements.

- Version constraints can specify exact versions, inclusive or exclusive bounds, conjunctions, alternatives, textual prefixes, compatible-major ranges and compatible-minor ranges. A conjunction requires every bound and an alternative requires at least one branch. A prefix constraint admits versions beginning with the supplied prefix. For numeric release versions, compatible ranges include their stated lower bound. Compatible-major ranges with at least one nonzero component stop before the next major version, except that a leading zero restricts compatibility at the first nonzero component; compatible-minor ranges stop before the next minor version when one is specified, otherwise before the next major version.

- For a version-range requirement with no pre-existing graph selection or lock constraint, Velmora selects the highest satisfying local version by default; if none exists, it uses the highest satisfying version from the first eligible remote offering a match. Requested updates consider eligible remotes as well as local versions and select the highest satisfying version. Prerelease versions are excluded unless enabled by the range. With prereleases enabled, an inclusive lower bound or exclusive upper bound stated as a release without qualifiers includes or excludes that release’s prereleases respectively; compatible ranges apply the same boundary rule. An explicit caller configuration enabling or disabling prereleases takes precedence over the range choice.

- Dependencies already selected for a shared visible requirement must satisfy subsequent constraints on that requirement. Incompatible versions or user/channel identities are refused as dependency conflicts. An explicit consumer override replaces the corresponding transitive version requirement; an override alone does not introduce an otherwise absent dependency.

- Ordinary dependencies use the host configuration. Build tools use the build configuration and can coexist with a different version of the same package in the host context. Test dependencies are available to the recipe performing the test and do not become requirements of its downstream consumers.

- Dependency declarations can independently control header use, library use, runtime use, propagation of headers and libraries, and visibility to downstream consumers. Consumers receive only the usage information enabled by those declarations; a non-visible dependency does not impose its version on an unrelated downstream dependency branch.

- A caller can register a local recipes index as a recipe source. Dependency resolution selects the indexed recipe folder for the requested version and makes its exported sources available for building; the index does not supply binary packages.

- Dependency cycles, including a package requiring itself, are refused as cyclic dependencies rather than producing an installable graph.

- Profile replacement declarations substitute matching recipe requirements, with separate declarations for ordinary and build-tool dependencies in each context. The first matching replacement wins, and its replacement reference is not replaced again. Later profiles replace earlier declarations for the same pattern and can remove inherited replacement declarations. Requirements supplied directly on the command line are not replaced; profile replacements take precedence over recipe-declared overrides.

- A recipe can reuse behavior and exported material from versioned Python recipes, including transitive reuse and declared version ranges. The resulting package behavior includes the selected reusable recipe behavior. Unresolvable reusable recipes and incompatible reusable recipe requirements are refused with the affected requirement identified.

- A platform-provided requirement satisfies an ordinary or test dependency without acquiring its recipe or binary package when its name agrees and its version satisfies the requested exact version or range. For an exact-version requirement, a recipe revision specified by both parties must agree. Build tools use the separate platform build-tool declarations. A nonmatching platform declaration leaves ordinary package resolution in effect.

- By default, an ordinary library dependency provides headers and libraries and is visible downstream. Shared libraries additionally provide runtime use; static and header libraries do not. Header libraries provide no link libraries. Applications and build scripts provide runtime use without headers or link libraries; build scripts are not visible downstream by default. Build-tool requirements default to runtime use without headers, link libraries or downstream visibility. Explicit usage declarations override these defaults.

- Without explicit propagation overrides, static libraries pass their library dependencies onward for linking but not their headers; shared libraries and applications pass runtime dependencies onward without exposing their private compile and link requirements. Header libraries pass their dependency headers and libraries onward. A dependency declared invisible is not propagated. Explicit header and library propagation choices control those uses for visible dependencies.

- Versions compare their main components in order, numerically when both components are integers and lexically otherwise. Trailing zero components do not distinguish versions. For equal main versions, prereleases precede releases, prerelease components use the same comparison, and build qualifiers distinguish otherwise equal versions using that comparison, with absence preceding a supplied qualifier.

### FP-06: Binary identity and declared compatibility

- By default, binary identity includes the resolved recipe's declared settings, option choices and applicable dependency identities. Repeating the same identity-relevant configuration preserves binary identity. Recipes can remove or normalize configuration dimensions so that the declared equivalent configurations reuse a binary.

- Recipes can choose dependency identity sensitivity: no dependency contribution; major-version sensitivity for nonzero major versions with exact versions for zero-major releases; major, minor, patch or fourth-component version sensitivity; complete recipe reference; recipe reference with recipe revision; recipe reference with binary identity; or recipe reference with both recipe revision and binary identity. Every contributing mode retains the dependency name, user and channel. For numeric release versions, a version-sensitive mode retains the named components and treats omitted components as zero. A change outside the selected sensitivity leaves the consumer binary identity unchanged, while a change in an included dimension changes it.

- By default, a static-library dependency embedded in an application or shared library contributes its complete recipe revision and binary identity. For a shared-library dependency of an application, shared library or static library, and for a static-library dependency of a static library, a patch-only release change preserves consumer identity while a minor release change changes it. Automatically configured header-only packages omit settings, options and dependencies from binary identity.

- Package revisions distinguish different packaged contents for the same recipe revision and binary identity. Repeated packaging of identical package contents preserves the package revision; explicit package-revision selection identifies the requested contents.

- A recipe can reject a binary configuration as invalid and supply a reason. Binary analysis makes that reason observable and distinguishes it from a valid configuration whose binary is missing.

- As an explicitly scoped alternative to exact binary matching, recipe-declared or installed compatibility policies may permit other settings or options. An available exact binary takes precedence; otherwise the first available candidate in the declared compatibility order can satisfy the request. A binary with no declared compatibility is not eligible merely because it exists.

- For a requested binary absent from the selected repositories, binary diagnostics can show available candidate configurations and their differing settings, options or dependencies, distinguishing absent binaries from candidates with a configuration mismatch.

- For direct dependencies providing headers or libraries, a header-library dependency contributes its complete recipe revision and binary identity to a static library, shared library or application. Ordinary dependencies of a header-library consumer do not contribute by default. For an unknown consumer kind, a nonzero dependency major version determines compatibility and a zero-major dependency uses its complete version. Dependencies providing neither headers nor libraries, build tools and test dependencies do not contribute by default. Reusable Python recipe dependencies contribute their major and minor version by default.

### FP-07: Build-system integration and dependency usage

- Given a configured dependency graph and available dependency artifacts, Velmora can generate dependency usage information for CMake, Meson, Autotools, Make, pkg-config, Bazel, Premake, Qbs and SCons. A consumer using that integration can find and use the selected dependency headers, libraries, compiler definitions and link requirements.

- Velmora can generate toolchain configuration for CMake, Meson, Autotools, Bazel, Premake and Qbs. Each integration carries caller-supplied compile and link flags into its native build inputs. CMake, Meson, Autotools and Qbs reflect the selected build configuration and target platform. Autotools Debug builds retain standard C and C++ assertion checks, while Release builds disable them. Bazel carries release or debug selection and caller-supplied compiler and target choices. Premake carries language-standard and shared-library choices.

- Component-aware integrations for CMake and pkg-config expose declared components and their dependency usage, including transitive libraries and system dependencies. Requesting a component whose declared required component is absent fails as an invalid dependency declaration.

- For CMake, dependency information for distinct build configurations can coexist, and the native build selects the configuration corresponding to its build type. Generating a second configuration does not substitute its dependency libraries for the first configuration.

- For CMake, Meson and Autotools cross-compilation using GCC or Clang, the generated build inputs describe the host target while build-tool requirements remain executable in the build context. Caller-selected sysroots, compiler executables and target flags are reflected in those native build inputs.

- Recipe-defined source, build and generated-output locations are honored by the build integration. Generation into a caller-selected output area yields usable native build inputs referring to the declared locations.

- Selecting an unknown generator is refused. Qbs generation requires a compiler selection and supports GCC and Clang; a missing or different compiler is refused, as is a compiler executable name that cannot be found by searching the execution environment.

### FP-08: Build and runtime environments

- For available dependencies, a build environment exposes executable locations and declared environment contributions of build tools and their runtime dependencies. A runtime environment exposes executable and shared-library locations and declared runtime contributions of host dependencies enabled for runtime use.

- Callers and recipes can define, unset, append and prepend environment values, with path additions retaining their declared order. Profile environment declarations override dependency-supplied definitions for the same variable.

- Generated activation material applies the selected environment to external commands, and deactivation restores the prior values and prior absence of affected variables. Independent build configurations retain independently usable environments.

- A recipe command executed with a selected environment observes that environment and the requested working directory. A non-successful external command is reported as a command execution failure, unless the recipe explicitly requests to receive and handle its result.

### FP-09: Dependency locking

- A caller can capture resolved host requirements, build requirements and reusable Python recipe requirements in a lockfile. Reusing the lockfile preserves the selected versions and recipe revisions even when newer eligible recipes become available.

- A lockfile discovered for the consumer is used automatically unless the caller disables automatic locking or supplies another lockfile. Under strict locking, an explicitly requested recipe revision must have a matching lock entry or resolution is refused as a lock conflict.

- Strict locked resolution refuses a requirement with no applicable lock entry. Explicit partial locking resolves requirements absent from the lockfile normally, including an explicitly requested revision absent from the lockfile, while retaining applicable entries for unpinned requirements. Writing the updated lockfile records newly resolved entries.

- Lockfiles can accumulate dependencies from different configurations, and merging lockfiles retains their recorded requirements. Cleaning an updated lockfile removes entries unused by the selected graph.

- Callers can add and remove explicit recipe references in host, build, reusable-recipe and configuration dependency categories. Adding a version range instead of a concrete version is refused as an invalid lock entry; malformed or unreadable lockfiles are refused as invalid lock inputs.

- When a lockfile contains multiple versions satisfying a requirement, the highest satisfying locked version is selected. When it contains multiple revisions of that version, the most recent recorded revision timestamp determines the selection. Host, build and reusable-recipe requirements are matched within their own locked categories.

### FP-10: Source preparation, package creation and validation

- Given a Python recipe and available declared prerequisites, source preparation materializes the source files specified by the recipe in its selected source location. Recipe source failures are reported with the affected recipe and underlying cause; a text-only consumer declaration cannot supply executable source preparation.

- Recipe source facilities can acquire a URL-supplied archive, at least in zip and gzip-compressed tar formats, extract its source contents, and apply recipe-supplied patches. An unavailable download or a patch that cannot be applied is reported as source preparation failure.

- For a source download with caller-supplied integrity digests, every supplied digest must match the complete downloaded archive or file bytes in their original order. Supported checks are MD5 as defined by RFC 1321 and SHA-1 and SHA-256 as defined by FIPS 180-4. A mismatch is refused as source integrity failure.

- Recipe source-control facilities can acquire a Git repository at a specified commit, branch or tag and record its repository location and commit for subsequent acquisition. Capturing a reproducible revision from a working tree with uncommitted changes in the selected capture scope is refused.

- A local build executes the recipe-defined build with its selected configuration, source location, build location and dependencies, producing the recipe-defined build outputs. Failed external build commands and recipe errors cause the build operation to fail with the original cause identified.

- Package creation executes the recipe-defined source, build and packaging behavior and registers the packaged artifacts under the resulting recipe revision, binary identity and package revision. Packaged file contents follow the recipe selection and remain available to consumers after creation.

- A caller can package prebuilt artifacts using the recipe packaging behavior without rebuilding those artifacts. The resulting package carries the chosen settings, options and dependency identity, and contains the files selected by the packaging recipe.

- A caller can exercise an existing package through a separate consumer test recipe; package creation also runs its associated consumer test when present unless the caller disables it. The test receives the selected package reference, and a failing build or test makes the overall operation fail even if package artifacts have already been created.

- Recipe-declared host system prerequisites apply when consuming the package; build-system prerequisites apply when building from source and when preparing a local consumer build. Their requested architectures follow the host and build contexts respectively, and a prerequisite failure causes the affected operation to fail.

### FP-11: Dependency installation and build planning

- Installation makes the resolved dependencies available using existing local binaries or matching binaries acquired from the selected remotes. With no recipe or caller policy authorizing a source build, a missing binary is refused and its package configuration is identified.

- Source-build policies can forbid all builds, build missing binaries, force matching packages, exclude matching packages, build missing binaries only for matching packages, or rebuild packages whose dependencies are being built. Exclusions from forced building take precedence over positive force-build patterns. Forbidding all builds cannot be combined with forced building, building all missing binaries or dependency-triggered rebuilding.

- A recipe can permit source building when its binary is missing or prohibit source building. An explicit caller prohibition on all builds takes precedence over recipe permission to build missing binaries. A recipe that prohibits source building requires an existing binary even when the caller requests rebuilding; absence of that binary is refused. A recipe policy demanding a build on every use is refused as unsupported.

- As a separate opt-in compatibility behavior, a caller can request building a declared compatible configuration when the requested configuration is invalid and no compatible binary exists. Only configurations admitted by the declared compatibility policy are eligible.

- When source building is authorized, installation obtains the required dependency binaries and produces selected missing or forced binaries through the recipe build behavior. A dependency acquisition or build failure makes installation fail with the affected package identified.

- Successful consumer installation produces the requested build-system inputs and build and runtime environments from the installed graph.

- When consuming a prebuilt package without rebuilding it, private dependencies with no propagated compile or runtime use need not have their binaries installed. Their missing binaries do not prevent consumption. If the package is rebuilt, those dependencies again require binaries according to the selected build policy.

- Binary graph inspection distinguishes available local binaries, remotely available binaries, packages selected for building, missing binaries and invalid configurations. Inspection does not build the binaries.

- Build planning produces a dependency-respecting order grouped by recipe or by individual binary configuration; each recipe, or each binary configuration, appears as one plan entry, including in merged plans. A reduced plan contains only work selected for building, still ordered after its remaining prerequisites. Plans from multiple configurations can be merged while preserving prerequisites; incompatible plan groupings and attempts to merge already reduced plans are refused.

- A caller can run a supplied command using installed recipe requirements, direct requirements or profile build-tool requirements. The command can use the host environment, build environment or both; both are active by default, with host executable selection taking precedence on a name collision. Installation failures prevent execution, and a missing executable or unsuccessful command is reported as execution failure.

### FP-12: Package discovery and reusable selections

- A caller can search recipe names locally or in selected remotes and distinguish the repository supplying each result. Pattern searches return matching identities without converting a successful empty match into a found recipe. A remote query failure remains distinguishable from a successful query with no matches.

- Package listing can select recipe versions, recipe revisions, binary identities and package revisions, either explicitly, by patterns or by latest-revision selection. It exposes the selected binaries and their settings and options. A missing exact reference is identified as missing rather than substituted with another reference.

- Binary queries combine setting and option conditions with conjunction, disjunction and grouping. Results contain only matching configurations; a malformed query is refused as an invalid selection.

- Local selections can be restricted to artifacts unused for longer than a caller-specified duration. Using a package as a dependency refreshes its last-use time. An age-based selection against a remote is refused as unsupported.

- A caller can save a package selection preserving its recipe revisions, binary identities, package revisions and repository association. A dependency graph report can produce selections restricted by recipe or binary status, and merging package lists retains the union of their selected artifacts without duplicating identical selections.

- For a selection of local packages, repository discovery identifies which selected remotes contain each corresponding revision. The result distinguishes packages found in a remote from packages not found in any selected remote.

- A caller can add and remove ordinary, build-tool and test dependencies in a local recipe. Removal is supported when the remaining recipe is valid Python. When adding a bare package name, the highest version in the first selected remote providing that name is used, or the highest local version in local-only mode. A supplied version is used without searching. Numeric major versions produce a range from the selected version inclusive to the next major version exclusive; a version whose major component is not numeric remains exact.

- Each dependency kind is handled separately: a package declared as one kind is neither present nor removed as another. Adding an already-declared dependency leaves the existing declaration intact and identifies it as already present. Removing an absent dependency leaves the recipe unchanged and identifies it as absent. An unavailable discovered package is identified as unavailable and is not added; a missing local recipe is refused.

### FP-13: Artifact transfer and prepared uploads

- Uploading selected recipe and binary revisions to a remote makes their recipe material, exported sources and package contents available to other clients. Recipe-only transfer omits binary packages. Pattern, configuration-query and saved-list selections determine the transferred artifacts.

- Ordinary upload skips revisions already available at the destination unless forced, and honors a recipe policy that omits binary uploads while allowing recipe uploads. Requested integrity checking refuses artifacts whose recorded contents no longer match their integrity information.

- Downloading a selected recipe or package revision restores its artifact contents locally without building it or installing its dependency graph. Requested exported sources are included. A missing selected artifact is refused as unavailable, and authentication, authorization and transport errors remain observable failures.

- Upload preparation returns a reusable selection of artifacts and the upload decisions for the selected destination without writing artifacts or metadata to that destination. It includes the material necessary to transfer declared exported sources, even when those sources must be acquired from another remote.

- Executing a fully prepared upload transfers the prepared artifacts according to the recorded decisions and produces no recipe-defined side effects, including effects of reusable Python recipes. The prepared artifact files must remain available in the home where preparation took place. Destination changes after preparation do not revise those decisions.

- A prepared selection is bound to its recorded remote. A different destination, or a selection containing a mixture of prepared and unprepared entries under a destination, is refused and no selected artifacts are uploaded. Ordinary unprepared local package lists remain usable for a normal upload.

- Callers can attach and transfer selected recipe and binary metadata independently of transferring package contents. Adding or updating metadata does not change the recipe or package revision. Empty upload selections transfer nothing and are identified as empty selections.

- As an extension to local configuration installation, a caller can install configuration supplied by a versioned Velmora package. Its configuration contents become active in the selected Velmora home, and the installed configuration reference and revision remain available for reproducible configuration selection.

### FP-14: Artifact deployment

- A caller can deploy complete package artifacts from the installed dependency graph into a chosen destination. Full artifact deployment includes available host and build dependency packages; direct artifact deployment includes available direct dependency packages from both host and build contexts.

- Runtime deployment selects runtime-enabled host dependencies, direct or transitive, and deploys their executables and shared libraries while preserving subdirectories within the declared runtime locations. Dependencies without packaged artifacts do not yield runtime files and are identified as unavailable for runtime deployment.

- Package-defined deployment can be selected by package-reference patterns. Matching recipes with deployment behavior produce their declared deliverables in the chosen destination; exclusion patterns take precedence over inclusion patterns.

- When a deployer supplies replacement package locations, generated CMake dependency information refers to those deployed artifacts. A deployed CMake consumer can build using the deployed dependency contents after the original managed copies are removed.

- Caller-supplied deployers can produce their declared deliverables from the dependency graph. An unavailable deployer, an unwritable destination or a failure in supplied deployment behavior causes deployment to fail with the affected cause identified.

### FP-15: Local artifact maintenance and recovery

- A caller can locate stored recipe material, sources, build results, package contents and metadata for a selected recipe or package revision. Missing exact revisions are refused as missing artifacts.

- Integrity checking distinguishes intact selected artifacts from artifacts with missing or changed recorded files or missing integrity information. It identifies the damaged recipes or packages and fails if any checked artifact is damaged.

- Selective removal deletes matching recipes, recipe revisions, binaries or package revisions locally or from a chosen authorized remote. Removing a recipe revision also removes its associated binaries; removing only selected binaries retains their recipe. Unselected artifacts remain available.

- A removal dry run identifies the artifacts selected for removal without deleting them. Caller confirmation is required for destructive selection unless confirmation has been supplied explicitly.

- Cleanup can remove selected source, build, download and source-backup material while retaining exported recipes and completed package artifacts. A cleanup selection does not remove unrelated package work.

- Saving selected artifacts produces a restorable archive containing their recipe and package revisions, package contents and metadata. Restoring that archive into another Velmora home preserves these artifacts and leaves unrelated existing artifacts available; repeating the restore does not create additional identities for the same revisions.

- A requested restore input that does not exist is refused as a missing archive.

### FP-16: Editable package development

- A caller can register a local Python recipe as an editable package and inspect or remove the resulting reference-to-working-directory mapping. Registration requires a package name and version; a reference pinned to a recipe revision is refused.

- For an exact reference registered as editable, consumer dependency resolution uses the registered working tree instead of a stored binary package. Its declared source and build locations provide consumer headers, libraries and component information, so local file changes are visible without repackaging.

- Editable versions participate in version-range selection alongside local stored versions. Without an update or lock constraint, the highest satisfying local candidate is selected.

- An editable package can have a caller-selected output area for generated and build material. Consumers use its declared editable layout and component locations. Removing the mapping returns subsequent resolution to ordinary recipe and binary selection without deleting the working tree.

### FP-17: Multi-package workspaces

- A caller can initialize a workspace, discover its root from within it, and add, inspect or remove member recipes. Membership maps package references to local recipe locations and optional output areas. A requested workspace that is not defined is refused.

- Workspace members behave as editable packages within that workspace without becoming global editable registrations.

- Workspace definitions can obtain member references from their recipes. Duplicate member references, entries with no determinable package reference, and recipes that cannot be loaded are refused as invalid workspace membership.

- Workspace source acquisition operates on selected members. Dependency installation, local builds and package creation operate in dependency-respecting order. Package creation requires explicitly selecting every workspace prerequisite that needs creation. A failed member dependency build prevents a successful aggregate result and identifies the failed operation.

- A caller can use a workspace lockfile for reproducible dependency selection. Workspace installation, build and combined installation retain that shared selection. These operations do not automatically adopt member lockfiles.

- Combined workspace installation treats member packages as one consumer and installs dependencies external to the workspace, producing the requested integration for the combined consumer.

- Workspace opening can bring referenced package sources into local editable development, and workspace completion can add intermediate packages lying on dependency paths between existing workspace members.

### FP-18: User extensions and package policy enforcement

- Installed validation hooks can inspect recipe and package information and reject an export, configuration, source operation, build or package operation. A policy rejection causes the affected operation to fail and identifies the responsible extension and reason. Configuration rejection prevents source, build and packaging work; source and build policies can reject before the corresponding recipe work begins, preventing that work and subsequent production. Package validation can inspect produced package contents and refuse successful completion.

- An installed profile policy can validate or transform the effective host and build profiles. Rejected profiles fail with the policy reason; accepted transformations are visible in the configuration used for package operations.

- A caller can install custom commands and subcommands that accept their declared inputs and produce their declared results through Velmora. An extension that cannot be loaded is identified as unavailable while unrelated built-in commands remain usable.

- A caller can select an installed custom generator to produce its declared consumer files from the resolved dependency information. A requested generator that is not available or fails during generation causes the corresponding generation operation to fail.

- A configured command wrapper can transform the external command executed for a recipe. The command that actually runs exhibits the wrapper-supplied behavior, and wrapper errors are reported as extension failures.

- Configured package-signing extensions can create signatures for selected package artifacts and verify artifacts during package acquisition. A verification rejection prevents successful acquisition and identifies the package verification failure; explicitly requested signing without a configured signing extension is refused.

### FP-19: Dependency provenance

- From a resolved dependency graph, Velmora can produce a software bill of materials in CycloneDX 1.4 or 1.6 describing selected recipe names, versions, recipe revisions, declared licenses, descriptions and dependency relationships. Build and test dependencies are excluded by default and can each be included explicitly.

- Caller-supplied component information can augment the corresponding component descriptions.

- Given two recipe versions or revisions, or corresponding local recipe locations, recipe comparison identifies added, removed and changed recipe files, exported material, patches and acquired sources. Unavailable recipes or a failure to acquire their sources causes comparison to fail rather than yield an empty difference report.

### FP-20: Self-hosted Velmora package service

- A self-hosted Velmora service accepts authorized recipe and binary uploads and makes their revisions, exported sources, package contents and metadata available to other Velmora clients. Stored artifacts remain available across service restarts.

- Clients can query matching recipes and package revisions, retrieve selected artifacts and remove authorized selected artifacts. A request for an absent exact artifact is reported as missing; deletion of one selected revision leaves unrelated revisions available.

- The service authenticates configured users and enforces separate read and write policies by recipe identity. Package access follows the corresponding recipe policy, and deletion requires write permission.

- Permission rules are considered in their configured order, and the first matching identity rule decides access. A rule can authorize named users, all authenticated users or anonymous access. With no matching authorization, an unauthenticated caller is refused as requiring authentication and an authenticated caller is refused as denied permission.

- Invalid user credentials do not establish authenticated access. Credentials issued before a caller changes the service authentication secret are refused afterward. An invalid permission rule is reported as invalid server configuration rather than granting access. Installed authentication and authorization extensions can supply the corresponding user and permission decisions.

